Z||000001||SLYGUY||12-23-2001||12:09 AM||||How are you bleeding it?<P>Do you have someone around to check to see just how much the slave cylinder is moving?<P>Are the master/slave cylinders mounted firmly?<P>Sly<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||next level trends prez||12-23-2001||12:31 AM||||i am bleding it te way archie v8 said to... and both my cylinders are tight... the slave moves almost an inch maybe even that far.. but its like it need to go further.. i dont understand||||reg||1
Z||000003||LZeitgeist||12-23-2001||10:04 AM||||Hmmmm... if the slave cylinder rod is getting an inch of movement, then it sounds like it's operating properly... I think the full throw on the slave is only about 15/16" or so (somebody please correct me if I'm wrong)... <P>Hmm. Sounds like the problem might lie somewhere besides the clutch hydraulics, but I don't know all the details of your situation at this moment...||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||12-23-2001||07:02 PM||||An inch is OK,you don't want much more,but that shouldn't hurt anything,however,you really need to measure accurately,as 7/8" won't work,and it'll do what you're describing.<BR>Have you checked the tranny fluid level?<BR>Have you checked out the shift linkeage for excess slop,or wear?<BR>The console lifts out and underneath is the shifter;there are some alignment holes that are put there so you can reset the cable adjustment.Just look for some tiny(1/8")holes,and put the car in whatever gear it takes to line them up,loosen the cables back at the gearbox with a drillbit or something through both the holes,and re-tighten the cables to the linkeage arms.Might make a difference,and everything counts with this system.||||reg||1
Z||000005||frankshel||12-23-2001||09:40 PM||||I have had the clutch problem before but corrected it for all time!!!  Are you readY??? You must have the old clutch rod from the old cylinder..if so, you need to make the rod a little longer. Mine has been fine for 7 years so....Take the old rod and with a hack saw, cut in two about 2 inches from the hole that hooks to the pedal. After you cut it, find a piece of metal about 2 1/2" to 3" long. Drill a hole in it from both ends leaving about 1/2" of thickness between the holes. Then all you have to do is slide it between the 2 pieces giving you extra throw on the slave. The depth of the holes keeps the rod from coming out, but if you like, after you feel comfortable that it adjusted right, fill the holes with epoxy. Believe me, it will never come apart. I have done over 10 of these for peole that I know and they have never had a problem. I leave mine in the holes loose and if I ever need to adjust it, I take a brass Bee Bee from a Bee Bee gun, drop it in one side, and bingo....i have all the stroke I need. Trust me...this is the best way to put it to rest...you copuld be fooling with it for weeks the other way. I have always cut the one alredy in the car because I am good at this fix, so rather than cutting the one that is out of the vehicle and putting it in, cut the one that is already in...What do you have to lose...you can always put the one that is out of the car back in if you have a problem.  If you would like to call me, my # is 518-585-7357 or at work  518-438-6977   Good luck...GUARANTEED TO WORK....and if anyone tells you that the throw out bearing will rub...DON'T BELIEVE THEM!!!!THERE IS PLENTY OF DISTANCE.....I HAVE THE PROOF!!!!!!!<BR>Frank Sheldon<BR>Ticonderoga, NY||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||12-23-2001||10:22 PM||||With the Fiero hydraulic system the TO bearing always touches the pressure plate. Nature of the system.||||reg||1
Z||000007||lowCG||12-24-2001||03:29 PM||||I did something similar on my 4cyl.car,but just watch to be sure that you don't get much over an inch of travel if you do try that one.<BR>Really sucks to tweak out your pressure plate from too much travel.||||reg||1
